# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is committed.
#
# Two kinds of value live in `.env`, and the difference is the prefix.
#
# 1. PUBLIC_* — public by design. The site is a static build, so anything
#    prefixed `PUBLIC_` is compiled into the client bundle and is visible to
#    anyone who opens devtools. Treat all three below as published the moment
#    you deploy.
#
#    That is safe only because of how the database is locked down: the anon
#    role has no privileges on `public.rsvps` at all, so the anon key below
#    cannot read, insert, update or delete anything. It exists so the admin
#    area can sign in. See supabase/migrations/0001_rsvps.sql before changing
#    that.
#
# 2. Unprefixed secrets — local only, and they belong here. Astro only inlines
#    `PUBLIC_`-prefixed variables into the bundle, so an unprefixed value in
#    `.env` is read by tooling on this machine and never reaches the browser.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This one belongs in your local `.env`. The admin proofs — reading a reply
# back out, checking a constraint actually bites, confirming the RSVP table is
# empty after a test — all need a key that bypasses RLS, and there is no way to
# run them without it. Supabase Edge Function secrets are where the *deployed*
# function gets its copy (injected by the platform, never set by hand); they
# are not somewhere a local script can read from.
#
# The three rules, in order of how badly each one ends:
#
#   · NEVER prefix it `PUBLIC_`. That single word is the difference between a
#     key on your disk and a key compiled into a static page served to every
#     guest. There is no recovering from that except rotation.
#   · NEVER commit it. `.env` is gitignored, and `.githooks/pre-commit` refuses
#     any staged `.env` file — including one forced in with `git add -f` — and
#     any staged line carrying `sb_secret_` or `service_role`. The hook is
#     wired up by `npm install`; see the README if it is not running.
#   · ROTATE IT BEFORE LAUNCH. It has been on a development machine, in shell
#     history and in tool output. Roll it in the Supabase dashboard once the
#     site is real, and treat the pre-launch value as spent.
#
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY is different and does NOT go here: nothing local needs
# it, only the deployed function does, so it lives solely in Edge Function
# secrets. See the README.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijklmnop.supabase.co
PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The `anon` / publishable key. Useless on its own by design.
PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Cloudflare Turnstile site key (the public half of the pair).
PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# Local only. Bypasses RLS entirely — full read/write on every table. Read the
# three rules above before filling this in. Leave blank if you are not running
# the admin proofs.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
